\section{DOI Resolution}
\label{sec:doi-resolution}

When users have a DOI but not a direct PDF URL, we need to resolve the DOI
to find an open access PDF. We try multiple sources in order of reliability:

\begin{description}
\item[Unpaywall] The primary source for legal open access PDFs. Unpaywall
  maintains a database of open access versions of papers.
\item[Semantic Scholar] Provides PDF links for many papers in their database.
\item[DOI redirect] As a fallback, following the DOI redirect sometimes leads
  to a page with a PDF link.
\end{description}

Finding a URL is not the same as getting a PDF: an open-access link can
be dead, and a publisher's link answers with a login page to anyone
outside a subscribing network.
So the sources do not compete to name \emph{the} URL.
Each contributes a candidate, and the download
(\cref{sec:pdf-download-doi}) works through them until one delivers.

\subsection{DOI Detection}

First, we need to detect whether an input string is a DOI. DOIs have a
standard format starting with \texttt{10.} followed by a registrant code
and a suffix.

<<doi resolution functions>>=
import re

DOI_PATTERN = re.compile(r'^10\.\d{4,}/[^\s]+$')
DOI_URL_PATTERN = re.compile(r'(?:https?://)?(?:dx\.)?doi\.org/(10\.\d{4,}/[^\s]+)')


def is_doi(identifier: str) -> bool:
    """
    Check if a string is a DOI.
    
    Accepts both raw DOIs (10.1234/example) and DOI URLs
    (https://doi.org/10.1234/example).
    
    Args:
        identifier: String to check.
    
    Returns:
        True if the string is a DOI or DOI URL.
    """
    identifier = identifier.strip()
    if DOI_PATTERN.match(identifier):
        return True
    if DOI_URL_PATTERN.match(identifier):
        return True
    return False


def extract_doi(identifier: str) -> str | None:
    """
    Extract the DOI from a string.
    
    Handles both raw DOIs and DOI URLs; a URL's percent-escapes are
    decoded once, a raw DOI is taken as it is.
    
    Args:
        identifier: String potentially containing a DOI.
    
    Returns:
        The extracted DOI, or None if not a DOI.
    """
    identifier = identifier.strip()
    if DOI_PATTERN.match(identifier):
        return identifier
    match = DOI_URL_PATTERN.match(identifier)
    if match:
        return unquote(match.group(1))
    return None
@

A DOI URL is a URL: what follows [[doi.org/]] is the DOI with its
percent-escapes, and the DOI is what they decode to.
Crossref's own example of resolving an old DOI with a [[#]] is
[[https://doi.org/10.1002/(SICI)1521-3951(199911)216:1<135::AID-PSSB135>3.0.CO;2-%23]]
(\url{https://www.crossref.org/documentation/member-setup/constructing-your-dois/suffixes-containing-special-characters/},
read 2026-11-23).
The DOI ends in [[2-#]], and every request encodes it again
(\cref{sec:identifier-in-path} in the network module); taken
undecoded, it went out as [[2-%2523]], a DOI that does not exist.
A raw DOI is not decoded: a [[%]] in it is a character of the DOI.
The PDF imports follow, since they choose their key through
[[extract_doi]] too (\cref{sec:pdf-import-key}): the URL and the DOI
it names share one key.
Before the decoding, an import by such a URL was keyed by the DOI as
written in it, escapes and all, so the lookups also try that key
([[doi_url_path]] keeps the written form, \cref{sec:pdf-import-key}).

<<doi resolution functions>>=
def doi_url_path(identifier: str) -> str | None:
    """Return the DOI of a DOI URL as written, escapes undecoded.

    None for anything but a DOI URL.
    """
    identifier = identifier.strip()
    if DOI_PATTERN.match(identifier):
        return None
    match = DOI_URL_PATTERN.match(identifier)
    return match.group(1) if match else None
@

\subsection{Unpaywall Resolution}

Unpaywall maps a DOI to its open-access locations.  The lookup itself lives in
the Unpaywall module (\cref{unpaywall-module}), which caches per DOI, encodes
the DOI, retries transient errors, and sends the contact email Unpaywall
requires; this function is a thin adapter that keeps the progress-callback
contract the other resolvers share.  Routing through that module rather than
calling the API here means a DOI whose session was already enriched opens
without a second request---and that, absent a configured [[SCHOLAR_EMAIL]],
resolution quietly falls through to Semantic Scholar instead of sending a
bogus address.

<<doi resolution functions>>=
def resolve_doi_unpaywall(
    doi: str,
    progress_callback: Callable[[str], None] | None = None,
) -> str | None:
    """
    Resolve a DOI to a PDF URL using Unpaywall.

    Requires a contact email (SCHOLAR_EMAIL); without one, returns None.

    Args:
        doi: The DOI to resolve.
        progress_callback: Optional callback for status messages.

    Returns:
        PDF URL if found, None otherwise.
    """
    logger.debug("Resolving DOI via Unpaywall: %s", doi)
    if progress_callback:
        progress_callback(f"Checking Unpaywall for {doi}...")
    url = resolve_pdf_url(doi)
    if url:
        logger.debug("Found PDF URL via Unpaywall")
    else:
        logger.debug("No Unpaywall PDF URL for DOI %s", doi)
    return url
@

\subsection{Semantic Scholar Resolution}

Semantic Scholar also provides PDF links for many papers. We query their
API using the DOI.
That is a request against the same API as the [[s2]] search provider, so
it goes through the provider's rate limiter (\cref{ch:ratelimit}): it is
paced with the searches and enrichment lookups, counted in
[[scholar providers limits]], and refused---quietly, the limiter has
said why---while a remembered cooldown lasts.
We take the limiter from the registered provider rather than by name,
so that it carries the provider's declared rate even when no search has
run in this process yet.
For the same reason the URL comes from the provider module's
[[s2_paper_url]], which percent-encodes the DOI
(\cref{sec:s2-paper-path} in the providers module).
This module used to fill in a template of its own,
[[.../paper/DOI:{doi}]], so a DOI holding a [[#]] asked about whatever
came before it.

<<doi resolution functions>>=
def resolve_doi_s2(
    doi: str,
    progress_callback: Callable[[str], None] | None = None,
) -> str | None:
    """
    Resolve a DOI to a PDF URL using Semantic Scholar.
    
    Args:
        doi: The DOI to resolve.
        progress_callback: Optional callback for status messages.
    
    Returns:
        PDF URL if found, None otherwise.
    """
    logger.debug("Resolving DOI via Semantic Scholar: %s", doi)
    if progress_callback:
        progress_callback(f"Checking Semantic Scholar for {doi}...")
    
    try:
        from scholar.providers import s2_paper_url

        limiter = _s2_limiter()
        limiter.acquire()
        url = s2_paper_url(f"DOI:{doi}")
        response = network.get(
            url,
            params={"fields": "openAccessPdf"},
            timeout=10,
        )
        limiter.record_response(response)

        if response.status_code != 200:
            logger.debug("Semantic Scholar returned status %d for DOI %s", 
                        response.status_code, doi)
            return None
        
        data = response.json()
        oa_pdf = data.get("openAccessPdf")
        if oa_pdf and oa_pdf.get("url"):
            logger.debug("Found PDF URL via Semantic Scholar")
            return oa_pdf["url"]
        
        logger.debug("No PDF URL found in Semantic Scholar response for DOI %s", doi)
        return None
        
    except Exception as e:
        logger.debug("Semantic Scholar request failed for DOI %s: %s", doi, e)
        return None


def _s2_limiter():
    """Return the Semantic Scholar provider's shared rate limiter."""
    from scholar.providers import get_provider

    provider = get_provider("s2")
    if provider is None:
        return get_limiter("s2")
    return limiter_for(provider)
@

Let's verify that the lookup is counted on the provider's limiter and
that a cooldown stops it before any request is sent.

<<test functions>>=
def test_resolve_doi_s2_passes_the_s2_limiter():
    """The PDF lookup is an s2 request like any other."""
    from scholar.ratelimit import get_limiter

    response = Mock(status_code=404, headers={})
    with patch("scholar.pdf.requests.get", return_value=response):
        assert resolve_doi_s2("10.1234/test") is None
    assert get_limiter("s2").status().day_count == 1

    get_limiter("s2").record_limit_hit(reason="rate limited (HTTP 429)")
    with patch("scholar.pdf.requests.get") as get:
        assert resolve_doi_s2("10.1234/test") is None
    get.assert_not_called()
