\section{Telling a readable PDF from anything else}
\label{sec:pdf-import-check}

[[download_pdf]] tells a PDF from an HTML page by the response's
[[Content-Type]] header; it never looks at the bytes.
That is not enough for what lies on disk.
A download cut off after a few hundred bytes (a dropped connection,
Ctrl-C) used to stay in the cache as a file that every later call
returned as a hit, and quote extraction then ended in PDFium's
traceback.
A file the user imports has no header at all, and guessing one from
the file name would accept exactly the case an import must refuse: the
bot-check page saved as [[paper.pdf]].
So both ask the question that matters for what follows: can PDFium,
the engine [[extract_pdf_pages]] reads the full text with
(\cref{sec:quote-extraction}), open the file, and does it have a page?
A file that passes is one [[pdf quote]] and the classification can
read.
PDFium is given the path, not the bytes: it reads what it needs, and a
large PDF is not held in memory for the check.

When PDFium refuses, the reason should point at the likely mistake.
A file that begins like an HTML page is probably the publisher's answer
saved instead of the PDF.
A file PDFium refuses for want of a password (PDFium's error code
[[FPDF_ERR_PASSWORD]], whose message reads \enquote{Incorrect password
error}) is encrypted, and the user can save an unencrypted copy.

<<pdf check functions>>=
def pdf_file_problem(path: Path) -> str | None:
    """
    Return why the file at `path` is not a readable PDF, or None.

    Readable means that PDFium (the engine `extract_pdf_pages` uses)
    opens it and finds at least one page.
    """
    import pypdfium2 as pdfium

    try:
        document = pdfium.PdfDocument(str(path))
    except pdfium.PdfiumError as error:
        if is_password_error(error):
            return "the PDF is encrypted; save an unencrypted copy"
        reason = f"PDFium cannot read it ({error})"
        if looks_like_html(path):
            reason += (
                "; it begins like an HTML page, such as a login or "
                "bot-check page"
            )
        return reason
    try:
        if len(document) == 0:
            return "it has no pages"
    finally:
        document.close()
    return None


def is_password_error(error: Exception) -> bool:
    """Whether PDFium refused a document for want of a password."""
    import pypdfium2.raw as pdfium_c

    code = getattr(error, "err_code", None)
    return code == pdfium_c.FPDF_ERR_PASSWORD or "password" in str(error)


def looks_like_html(path: Path) -> bool:
    """Whether the file at `path` begins like an HTML document."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            head = f.read(HTML_SNIFF_BYTES)
    except OSError:
        return False
    head = head.lstrip(b"\xef\xbb\xbf \t\r\n").lower()
    return head.startswith((b"<!doctype html", b"<html")) or b"<html" in head
<<pdf constants>>=
HTML_SNIFF_BYTES = 1024
@

A readable PDF may still hold no text: a scan is pictures of pages.
Quote extraction finds nothing in it, so the import warns about it
(\cref{sec:pdf-import-write}); the first page with any text answers
the question, so a PDF with text costs one page.

<<pdf check functions>>=
def pdf_has_text(path: Path) -> bool:
    """Whether any page of the readable PDF at `path` yields text."""
    import pypdfium2 as pdfium

    document = pdfium.PdfDocument(str(path))
    try:
        for page in document:
            textpage = page.get_textpage()
            text = textpage.get_text_range()
            textpage.close()
            page.close()
            if text.strip():
                return True
        return False
    finally:
        document.close()
