A cached file is used only when it is readable.
One that is not is treated as absent, with a warning naming the file
and the reason, so the caller downloads again or tries the next
candidate instead of handing PDFium a file it cannot read.
This is what retires the truncated files that older versions of
scholar left behind.
A caller that only peeks, before a call that checks the same file
again, passes [[warn=False]], so the warning appears once per call
([[get_pdf]] before [[download_pdf]], \cref{sec:pdf-download}).

<<pdf check functions>>=
def usable_cached_pdf(path: Path, warn: bool = True) -> bool:
    """
    Whether `path` exists and is a readable PDF to use as it is.

    An unreadable file is logged as ignored unless `warn` is false.
    """
    if not path.exists():
        return False
    problem = pdf_file_problem(path)
    if problem:
        if warn:
            logger.warning(
                "Ignoring the cached PDF %s: %s", path.name, problem
            )
        return False
    return True
@

Both a download and an import write their PDF to a temporary file
beside its place in the cache and rename it into place only when it is
complete.
A rename within one directory replaces the file in one step, so a
reader finds either the old file or the whole new one, and an
interrupted download leaves nothing under the key: the temporary file
is removed on the way out, also on Ctrl-C.
We do not use [[atomic_write_bytes]] (\cref{sec:atomic-write}) here,
because it takes the content as bytes, and a PDF is better streamed to
disk than held in memory.
The temporary names start with a dot and end in [[.tmp]], so the
[[*.pdf]] globs never see them.

<<pdf check functions>>=
@contextmanager
def temp_beside(target: Path):
    """Yield a new temporary path next to `target`; removed on exit."""
    fd, name = tempfile.mkstemp(
        dir=target.parent, prefix=f".{target.name}.", suffix=".tmp"
    )
    os.close(fd)
    tmp = Path(name)
    try:
        yield tmp
    finally:
        tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def sync_file(path: Path) -> None:
    """Flush the file at `path` to disk before it is renamed into place."""
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        os.fsync(f.fileno())
@

\section{PDF Download}
\label{sec:pdf-download}

The download function handles HTTP requests with proper error handling and
content-type validation. We verify the response is actually a PDF before
saving it.

Every request in this module goes through [[network.get]]
(\cref{ch:network}), for two reasons that matter more here than
anywhere else.
The route: a publisher that admits subscribers by address serves the
PDF only to a request that leaves from the subscribing network, so
[[--proxy]] or [[SCHOLAR_PROXY]] must reach the download, not just the
searches.
And the name: publishers that admit scripts at all turn away the
[[python-requests]] default, which is why the downloads used to fail
through a working campus tunnel while [[curl]] succeeded.
Scholar's own User-Agent is honest about being a tool; where a publisher
answers it with a Cloudflare challenge or a login page, the download
fails with the content-type check below, and that is where it stops:
we do not imitate a browser to get past a publisher's refusal.

A cached file counts as a hit only when PDFium can read it
([[usable_cached_pdf]], \cref{sec:pdf-import-check}); a truncated or
otherwise broken one is downloaded again.
The body is streamed to a temporary file and renamed into place when it
is complete, so a response that breaks off, or a Ctrl-C, leaves no
file under the key.

The temporary file is checked the same way before it is put in place.
A body that PDFium cannot read (an encrypted publisher PDF, an HTML
page served as [[application/pdf]]) is not cached and the download
fails with the reason.
Cached, it would never be a hit, so every call would fetch it again;
and since the same body would come again, the failure is remembered
for the rest of the process ([[UNREADABLE_DOWNLOADS]], keyed by the
cache file, so a test's own cache directory is a key of its own): a
batch that meets the paper again is told the reason without another
request.
It is remembered in memory only, not on disk: a later run tries again,
in case the publisher serves something else by then.
Only this failure is remembered.
A refused status, a dropped connection or a timeout may well pass on
the next try, and the TUI user who retries a download after a network
hiccup should get a new request.

<<pdf download functions>>=
class PDFDownloadError(Exception):
    """
    Exception raised when PDF download fails.
    
    Contains a descriptive error message suitable for display to users.
    """
    pass


class PDFUnreadableError(PDFDownloadError):
    """A PDF was had but PDFium cannot read it."""


UNREADABLE_DOWNLOADS: dict[Path, str] = {}
"""Cache files whose download was not a readable PDF, with the reason."""


def download_pdf(
    url: str,
    progress_callback: Callable[[str], None] | None = None,
) -> Path:
    """
    Download a PDF to the cache if not already cached.
    
    Args:
        url: URL of the PDF to download.
        progress_callback: Optional callback for status messages.
    
    Returns:
        Path to the cached PDF file.
    
    Raises:
        PDFDownloadError: If download fails with descriptive message.
    """
    cache_path = get_cached_pdf_path(url)
    
    if usable_cached_pdf(cache_path):
        logger.debug("PDF cache hit: %s", cache_path.name)
        if progress_callback:
            progress_callback(f"Using cached PDF: {cache_path}")
        return cache_path
    if cache_path in UNREADABLE_DOWNLOADS:
        raise PDFDownloadError(
            f"{UNREADABLE_DOWNLOADS[cache_path]} (tried earlier in this run)"
            f"\nURL: {url}"
        )
    
    logger.info("Downloading PDF from %s", url)
    if progress_callback:
        progress_callback(f"Downloading PDF from {url}...")
    
    try:
        response = network.get(url, timeout=30, stream=True)
        response.raise_for_status()
        
        # Validate content type
        content_type = response.headers.get("content-type", "")
        if "pdf" not in content_type.lower() and "octet-stream" not in content_type.lower():
            logger.warning("URL did not return PDF content-type: %s", content_type)
            raise PDFDownloadError(
                f"URL did not return a PDF. Content-Type: {content_type}\nURL: {url}"
            )
        
        <<stream the response into the cache atomically>>
        
        logger.debug("PDF downloaded and cached at %s", cache_path.name)
        if progress_callback:
            progress_callback("Download complete")
        
        return cache_path
        
    except requests.exceptions.HTTPError as e:
        logger.warning("HTTP error downloading PDF: %s %s", 
                      e.response.status_code, e.response.reason)
        raise PDFDownloadError(
            f"HTTP error {e.response.status_code}: {e.response.reason}\nURL: {url}"
        )
    except requests.exceptions.ConnectionError as e:
        logger.warning("Connection error downloading PDF: %s", e)
        raise PDFDownloadError(f"Connection error: {e}\nURL: {url}")
    except requests.exceptions.Timeout:
        logger.warning("Timeout downloading PDF from %s", url)
        raise PDFDownloadError(f"Request timed out after 30 seconds\nURL: {url}")
    except requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:
        logger.warning("Request failed downloading PDF: %s", e)
        raise PDFDownloadError(f"Request failed: {e}\nURL: {url}")
