\subsection{Testing download functions}

Since a cached file is used only when PDFium can read it
(\cref{sec:pdf-import-check}), tests that put a file in the cache need
real PDFs: [[b"%PDF-1.4"]], which the older tests write as a stand-in,
is refused by PDFium as it should be.
[[make_pdf]] writes one by hand, a page with an optional line of text
in a standard font, the cross-reference table computed from the
objects' offsets.
It can also mark the file encrypted: an [[/Encrypt]] dictionary of the
standard handler with made-up password hashes, which PDFium refuses for
want of the password, exactly as it refuses an encrypted PDF whose user
password is not empty.
(PDFium can write a blank PDF itself, but neither text nor encryption.)
Two page widths give two PDFs with different content.
A download is checked the same way before it is cached, so the
stand-in responses of the download tests serve these bytes too
([[pdf_document]]).

<<test functions>>=
def make_pdf(path, width=200, text=None, encrypted=False):
  """Write a real one-page PDF, optionally with a line of `text`."""
  path.write_bytes(pdf_document(width, text, encrypted))
  return path


def pdf_document(width=200, text=None, encrypted=False):
  """The bytes of a real one-page PDF, as `make_pdf` writes them."""
  content = b""
  if text:
    content = b"BT /F1 12 Tf 20 100 Td (%s) Tj ET" % text.encode()
  objects = [
    b"@<< /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R >>",
    b"@<< /Type /Pages /Kids [3 0 R] /Count 1 >>",
    b"@<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 %d 200] /Contents 4 0 R"
    b" /Resources @<< /Font @<< /F1 5 0 R >> >> >>" % width,
    b"@<< /Length %d >>\nstream\n%s\nendstream" % (len(content), content),
    b"@<< /Type /Font /Subtype /Type1 /BaseFont /Helvetica >>",
  ]
  trailer = b""
  if encrypted:
    objects.append(
      b"@<< /Filter /Standard /V 1 /R 2 /P -4 /O <%s> /U <%s> >>"
      % (b"11" * 32, b"22" * 32)
    )
    trailer = b"/Encrypt 6 0 R /ID [<%s> <%s>] " % (b"ab" * 16, b"ab" * 16)
  data = b"%PDF-1.4\n"
  offsets = []
  for number, body in enumerate(objects, 1):
    offsets.append(len(data))
    data += b"%d 0 obj\n%s\nendobj\n" % (number, body)
  xref = len(data)
  data += b"xref\n0 %d\n0000000000 65535 f \n" % (len(objects) + 1)
  data += b"".join(b"%010d 00000 n \n" % offset for offset in offsets)
  data += b"trailer\n@<< /Size %d /Root 1 0 R %s>>\nstartxref\n%d\n%%%%EOF\n" % (
    len(objects) + 1, trailer, xref
  )
  return data
@

