A DOI given as a [[doi.org]] URL is decoded once
(\cref{sec:doi-resolution}), and before that it was not: an import by
[[--url https://doi.org/10.5555/(SICI)abc;2-%23]] was stored under the
key of [[10.5555/(sici)abc;2-%23]], escapes and all, while the same
URL now names the DOI [[10.5555/(SICI)abc;2-#]] and its key.
So every lookup of a DOI that came from such a URL tries both keys,
the decoded one first: [[doi_pdf_paths]] takes the DOI as written in
the URL beside the decoded DOI ([[doi_url_path]]) and adds its key when
it differs.
A new import goes under the decoded key; one that finds a copy under
the old key and none under the new one goes to the old key instead
(\cref{sec:pdf-import-write}), so the same PDF is \enquote{unchanged},
another needs [[--replace]], and there are never two copies of one
paper.

<<pdf cache functions>>=
def doi_pdf_paths(doi: str, written: str | None = None) -> list[Path]:
    """The cache paths a PDF imported for `doi` may have, newest key first.

    `written` is the DOI as a doi.org URL wrote it, before decoding; its
    key, used by imports before the URL was decoded, comes second.
    """
    paths = [get_cached_doi_pdf_path(doi)]
    if written:
        old = get_cached_doi_pdf_path(written)
        if old != paths[0]:
            paths.append(old)
    return paths
@

\subsection{Copying, recording and refusing to overwrite}
\label{sec:pdf-import-write}

The file is first copied to a temporary file beside its place in the
cache ([[temp_beside]], \cref{sec:pdf-import-check}), and the check
and the hash are made on that copy.
So the bytes checked are the bytes that end up in the cache, even if
the user's file changes meanwhile, and a large PDF is streamed rather
than held in memory: [[shutil.copyfile]] copies in blocks,
[[file_sha256]] hashes in blocks, and PDFium opens the path.
The user's file is only read, never moved or changed.

Each import has a record beside the copy, [[<hash>.import.json]]: the
SHA-256 and size of the content, the time of the import (UTC), the
file's base name, the [[--from]] text that says where it came from, the
URL or DOI it was imported for as given, and for a DOI the normalised
form it is stored under (\cref{sec:pdf-import-key}).
JSON of built-in types, so any later version can read it.
Only the base name is stored, not the directory the user typed: a path
says more about the user than the record needs.
The record marks the copy as imported only while its SHA-256 is the
file's (\cref{sec:pdf-cache-imports}).

An existing readable copy under the key with other content is not
overwritten unless the caller says [[replace]]: it may be a download
that works, or an earlier import whose source is gone.
The error says which of the two it is and names the hash that is there,
so the user can tell which file it was.
A copy PDFium cannot read is replaced without asking: it is what a
broken download leaves, and most likely the reason for the import.
The same content is not copied again; if that copy has no record yet
(a download of the same file), the record is added, since the user has
now said that this copy matters.

A readable PDF without any text (a scan) is imported, since it is the
user's to keep, but with a warning: quote extraction and the full-text
classification will find nothing in it.

Two processes importing for the same key at once would each find no
copy, each write, and leave whichever finished last.
The compare-and-write therefore runs under the cache's lock
([[pdf_cache_lock]], \cref{sec:pdf-cache-imports}), so the second
import sees the first one's copy and is refused or recognised as the
same; and the PDF is renamed into place whole, so no reader ever sees
half of it.
The record is written before the PDF.
If the process dies between the two, what is left is a record without
its PDF, which marks nothing; the other order could leave an imported
PDF without its record, which the next [[clear_pdf_cache]] would delete
as a download.
An import that finds such an orphan under its key drops it first, so
it cannot be mistaken for the record of whatever lies there.

<<pdf import functions>>=
class PDFImportError(Exception):
    """A file that `import_pdf` refuses; the message says why."""


@dataclass
class PDFImport:
    """
    The outcome of `import_pdf`.

    `status` is "imported", "replaced", "recorded" (same content was
    cached, without a record) or "unchanged" (same content, already
    imported). `warnings` says what the user should know about the
    file, such as a PDF without text.
    """

    path: Path
    record: dict
    status: str
    warnings: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)


def import_pdf(
    file: str | os.PathLike,
    *,
    url: str | None = None,
    doi: str | None = None,
    source: str | None = None,
    replace: bool = False,
) -> PDFImport:
    """
    Copy a local PDF into the cache under the key of `url` or `doi`.

    The file is checked to be a PDF, copied (never moved) and recorded
    with its SHA-256, size, import time, base name, `source` and the
    URL or DOI given. A different readable PDF already cached under the
    key is replaced only with `replace`.

    Raises:
        ValueError: Unless exactly one of `url` and `doi` is given.
        PDFImportError: If the file cannot be read, is not a PDF, the
            DOI is not one, or another PDF is cached under the key.
    """
    <<choose the key of an import>>
    with temp_beside(cache_path) as tmp:
        <<copy the file to import and check the copy>>
        record = {
            "sha256": digest,
            "size": tmp.stat().st_size,
            "imported_at": utc_now_iso(),
            "file_name": Path(file).name,
            "from": source,
            "url": url,
            "doi": doi,
            "stored_doi": stored_doi,
        }
        record_path = import_record_path(cache_path)
        with pdf_cache_lock():
            if record_path.exists() and import_record_of(cache_path) is None:
                record_path.unlink()
            <<compare with the copy already cached>>
            atomic_write_text(
                record_path, json.dumps(record, indent=2) + "\n"
            )
            os.replace(tmp, cache_path)
    logger.info("Imported %s as %s", record["file_name"], cache_path.name)
    return PDFImport(cache_path, record, status, warnings)
